msgid "X86/X86-64 Language Extensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5469
msgid "The X86 backend has these language extensions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5471
msgid "Memory references to specified segments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5473
msgid ""
"Annotating a pointer with address space #256 causes it to be code generated "
"relative to the X86 GS segment register, address space #257 causes it to be "
"relative to the X86 FS segment, and address space #258 causes it to be "
"relative to the X86 SS segment. Note that this is a very very low-level "
"feature that should only be used if you know what you're doing (for example "
"in an OS kernel)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5480
msgid "Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5489
msgid "Which compiles to (on X86-32):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5498
msgid ""
"You can also use the GCC compatibility macros `__seg_fs` and `__seg_gs` for "
"the same purpose. The preprocessor symbols `__SEG_FS` and `__SEG_GS` "
"indicate their support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5502
msgid "PowerPC Language Extensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5504
msgid "Set the Floating Point Rounding Mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5506
msgid ""
"PowerPC64/PowerPC64le supports the builtin function `__builtin_setrnd` to "
"set the floating point rounding mode. This function will use the least "
"significant two bits of integer argument to set the floating point rounding "
"mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5514
msgid "The effective values for mode are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5516
msgid "0 - round to nearest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5517
msgid "1 - round to zero"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5518
msgid "2 - round to +infinity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5519
msgid "3 - round to -infinity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5521
msgid ""
"Note that the mode argument will modulo 4, so if the integer argument is "
"greater than 3, it will only use the least significant two bits of the mode. "
"Namely, `__builtin_setrnd(102))` is equal to `__builtin_setrnd(2)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5525
msgid "PowerPC cache builtins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5527
msgid ""
"The PowerPC architecture specifies instructions implementing cache "
"operations. Clang provides builtins that give direct programmer access to "
"these cache instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5531
msgid "Currently the following builtins are implemented in clang:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5533
msgid ""
"`__builtin_dcbf` copies the contents of a modified block from the data cache "
"to main memory and flushes the copy from the data cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5549
msgid "z/OS Language Extensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5551
msgid "z/OS builtins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5553
msgid ""
"z/OS supports builtins for compare-and-swap operations that generate the "
"corresponding z/OS assembly instructions (CS, CSG, CDSG). These builtins "
"compare the value pointed to by oldptr to the value pointed to by curptr. If "
"they are equal, the value pointed to by newword (or newword itself for "
"`__cs`) is copied into the location pointed to by curptr. If they are "
"unequal, the value pointed to by curptr is copied into the location pointed "
"to by oldptr. The builtins return 0 if the values are equal, or 1 if they "
"are unequal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5561
msgid ""
"`int __cs(unsigned int *oldptr, unsigned int *curptr, unsigned int newword)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5563
msgid ""
"Generates a 4-byte compare-and-swap using the CS instruction. Use `__cs` "
"instead of `__cs1` when the newword arg is an r-value instead of an l-value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5567
msgid "`int __cs1(void *oldptr, void *curptr, void *newword)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5569
msgid "Generates a 4-byte compare-and-swap using the CS instruction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5571
msgid "`int __csg(void *oldptr, void *curptr, void *newword)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5573 ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5577
msgid "Generates an 8-byte compare-and-swap using the CSG instruction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5575
msgid "`int __cds1(void *oldptr, void *curptr, void *newword)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5579
msgid "`int __cdsg(void *oldptr, void *curptr, void *newword)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5581
msgid "Generates a 16-byte compare-and-swap using the CDSG instruction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5583
msgid "Extensions for Static Analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LanguageExtensions.md:5585
msgid ""
"Clang supports additional attributes that are useful for documenting program "
"invariants and rules for static analysis tools, such as the [Clang Static "
"Analyzer](https://clang-analyzer.llvm.org/). These attributes are documented "
"in the analyzer's {doc}`list of annotations for analysis <analyzer/user-docs/"
"Annotations>`."
msgstr ""

